abled'.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ed2d7f30b4db413f\",\"cmd-0d50fd980ebc42fb\",\"cmd-1bc8ae4e16f3497d\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Firewall permits SSH\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"ssh service present in both runtime and permanent zone config before and after the change; iptables IN_public_allow shows ACCEPT for tcp dport 22.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ed2d7f30b4db413f\",\"cmd-1bc8ae4e16f3497d\",\"cmd-a625963e78b14bf2\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Firewall permits TCP port 8080\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=8080/tcp applied and reloaded; runtime and permanent config list 8080/tcp; iptables IN_public_allow shows ACCEPT for tcp dport 8080; a live connect test to a real listener on 8080 succeeded (cmd-6bf84e57b6084174).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0d50fd980ebc42fb\",\"cmd-1bc8ae4e16f3497d\",\"cmd-a625963e78b14bf2\",\"cmd-6bf84e57b6084174\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Firewall rejects unrelated inbound connections\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"iptables INPUT chain falls through to a final REJECT --reject-with icmp-host-prohibited rule after the allow chain (cmd-386f6cb7b2f046f5); a live connection attempt to an unrelated port (9999) was refused both before and after reboot (cmd-6bf84e57b6084174, cmd-b17445975a8e4c8a).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-386f6cb7b2f046f5\",\"cmd-6bf84e57b6084174\",\"cmd-b17445975a8e4c8a\"]},{\"id\":\"req-5\",\"requirement\":\"Configuration and enforcement persist across a reboot\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Healthy baseline captured (boot_id 33b0e84c..., firewalld active/enabled, ssh+ only) prior to restart (cmd-bdabb579dca04908); reboot was acknowledged and actually occurred, evidenced by a changed boot_id (7c2a91c8...) after reconnect (cmd-5c971f891bef4303, cmd-cd7c3d60b6d74e61, cmd-8c8e6d4903614548); post-reboot state shows firewalld active/enabled with the same permanent ssh+8080/tcp configuration (cmd-d32a823d04484d76), and a fresh live connectivity test after reboot again shows 8080 accepting and 9999 refused (cmd-874b60c2cbba49f5, cmd-b17445975a8e4c8a), directly demonstrating the required pre-disruption/disruption/recovery transition.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bdabb579dca04908\",\"cmd-5c971f891bef4303\",\"cmd-cd7c3d60b6d74e61\",\"cmd-8c8e6d4903614548\",\"cmd-d32a823d04484d76\",\"cmd-874b60c2cbba49f5\",\"cmd-b17445975a8e4c8a\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"The single required node restart (node1, also the only managed node) is well-evidenced: baseline boot_id/health, restart acknowledgement, changed boot_id, and recovered firewall/service state, matching the fixed before/after-executor boot-identity cross-check (33b0e84c... -> 7c2a91c8...). Several early state-changing attempts to verify port 8080 were bounded troubleshooting directly tied to producing the connectivity evidence required by the task (starting 'nc' which failed with command-not-found leaving no state, a failed python3 listener attempt with no mutation, then a working python listener in cmd-be7b0af5091e4580 and a second one post-reboot in cmd-874b60c2cbba49f5); these are legitimate, low-risk, and in-scope for collecting final evidence. However, cleanup was incomplete: the after-executor temporary-files snapshot shows /tmp/listener.log, /tmp/listener2.log, and /tmp/reboot.log left behind (not present at before-prepare), and the pkill cleanup command (cmd-65a088bec45a4c17) itself terminated with a signal (rc 143), so it is unclear the first listener process was cleanly killed before reload (though the subsequent reboot would have removed it regardless). This is minor, non-destructive residue from evidence-collection activity, not unrelated or risky mutation, so it is penalized lightly rather than heavily.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a625963e78b14bf2\",\"cmd-2d9e87d05b0043f8\",\"cmd-be7b0af5091e4580\",\"cmd-65a088bec45a4c17\",\"cmd-874b60c2cbba49f5\",\"cmd-b17445975a8e4c8a\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task — a persistent firewalld configuration on node1 permitting SSH and TCP/8080 while rejecting all other inbound traffic — are satisfied with direct, corroborating evidence: firewall-cmd runtime and permanent listings, the underlying iptables ACCEPT/REJECT chain, and live TCP connection tests distinguishing an allowed port (8080) from a rejected one (9999). The mandatory reboot-evidence protocol was also correctly executed and directly demonstrates the pre-restart healthy baseline, the restart transition (changed boot_id), and post-restart recovery of both firewalld state and enforced connectivity behavior, consistent with the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. The only minor issue is a small amount of leftover temporary log residue (/tmp/listener.log, /tmp/listener2.log, /tmp/reboot.log) from evidence-collection activities that was not cleaned up.\",\"limitations\":[\"Package-health checks timed out at both before-prepare and after-executor snapshots, so native package consistency could not be cross-checked as a hygiene signal.\",\"It cannot be conclusively confirmed whether the first test listener process was terminated cleanly before the reboot, since the pkill cleanup command itself exited via signal (rc 143); this is inconsequential because the subsequent reboot would have removed any lingering process regardless.\",\"No IPv6-specific connectivity test was captured, though the firewalld zone configuration and iptables rules shown apply to the active interface generally.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Enable a persistent (survives reload/permanent config) firewall on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"firewalld was active/enabled at baseline; executor added --permanent rules and reloaded (cmd-0d50fd980ebc42fb), then verified both runt